hermes v0.23.0: a delegate_task subagent gets a local shell of its own.

Symptom: parallel subagents (and their parent) on the local terminal
backend clobber each other's shell state. An `export X` in one child
shows up in a sibling; a child's `unset` is undone by a sibling's command
that finishes later; a leaked `export HOME=...` breaks the parent.

Cause: every delegated child resolves to the parent's environment key
(`session:<key>` / `default`), so all share ONE LocalEnvironment and its
bash snapshot file, which each command re-dumps with `export -p` when it
ends: the last writer wins.

Fix: on the local backend a child (delegated_child_context) runs in an
environment of its own, keyed `<parent key>::child::<task id>`, whose
snapshot starts as a copy of the top-level session's (same env, functions,
aliases). cwd was already per task. The child's environment is released when
the child finishes (release_child_env; a timed-out worker's once it is
done), otherwise by the idle reaper; its background jobs are unaffected.
File tools keep running in the parent's environment (paths from the
child's own cwd record), so they do not see a child's exports.
Container backends keep the shared sandbox (#16177). Check:
hyperhermes/checks/subagent-shell.

